
# =============================================================================
# mwan3 member defaults for the Z8102AX V2 (maxmodem build).
#
# flush_conntrack policy -- ONE rule for EVERY member below, v4 and v6 alike:
# 'ifup' and 'ifdown' only, never the tracker's connected/disconnected.
#
# mwan3_flush_conntrack() runs `echo f > nf_conntrack_flush`, which is a
# GLOBAL kernel flush, not a per-member one: the interface argument only
# selects whose list gets tested, but on a match the whole conntrack table
# is wiped for the entire router. One member's mistaken event therefore
# costs every OTHER member its live sessions too.
#
# The four possible events split cleanly by source of truth:
#   ifup / ifdown     -> the kernel really changed the link. Trustworthy.
#   connected /
#   disconnected      -> mwan3track's ping opinion. Fallible, and on this
#                        hardware routinely wrong: the carrier rotates the
#                        delegated /64 on renew, so the tracker can fail ALL
#                        targets mid-rotation while the link is perfectly
#                        fine.
#
# Hence flush only on kernel-truth events. NOT flushing on a real transition
# leaves stale NAT that ages out on its own within minutes; flushing on a
# false one drops every live session on the box. The second cost is not
# cheaply avoidable in order to avoid the first.
#
# Member enablement: this is the factory default for the members that ship
# here. A box configured for a different modem count gets its extra members
# enabled at boot: initialize.sh walks wan1..wan$maxmodem and, for every
# mwan3.wanN section that exists, sets both wanN and wanN_6 enabled=1. It does
# NOT enumerate members live -- a member it does not name stays as shipped.
#
# Default policy is 'balanced', not the stock ROOter 'failover'. Both policies
# are declared below and either can be selected from LuCI. The distinction is
# the whole point of the box: 'failover' keeps every connection on ONE member
# and only moves to the next when the current one drops, so four plugged-in
# modems idle doing nothing; 'balanced' spreads live traffic over every member
# that is up, which is what a multi-WAN cellular box is for. Verified live on
# a 2-modem deployment before the 3-5 modem members were added here.
# =============================================================================

